 [17592186476902 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476902 #edb/ref 1071 "The linear transformation $T$ has the standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the respective images of the standard basis vectors $\\{i,j,k\\}$ of ${R}^{3}$ under the action of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476902 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ebff972-8ab2-5583-88cb-dd4e3c35dfe2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476902 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nThe images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{i,j,k\\}$ are the first, second, and third columns of $T,$ respectively. \n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(j) & =T[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(k) & =T[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{i,j,k\\}$ by computing the matrix products $T\\cdot i,$ $T\\cdot j,$ and $T\\cdot k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T\\cdot i=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(j) & =T\\cdot j=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(k) & =T\\cdot k=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476928 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476928 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43832" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476928 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476928 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $\\{i,j\\}$ is the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ and that the images of $i$ and $j$ under the action of a linear transformation $T$ are $⟨1,-1⟩$ and $⟨2,3⟩$, respectively, what is the standard matrix of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476928 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d925f8f-8f71-5849-ad7a-d04b18e0b1d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476928 #edb/ref 1074 "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for this linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476951 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476951 #edb/ref 1069 "q-77955" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476951 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476951 #edb/ref 1071 "The linear transformation $T$ has the standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat are the respective images of the standard basis vectors $\\{i,j\\}$ of ${R}^{2}$ under the action of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476951 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b9c1cac7-603f-5a47-87fd-f2ee952aaad9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476951 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nThe images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{i,j\\}$ are the first and second columns of $T,$ respectively. \n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(j) & =T[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{i,j\\}$ by computing the matrix products $T\\cdot i$ and $T\\cdot j:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T\\cdot i=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(j) & =T\\cdot j=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
